Reviewer NOTE on the r3 verdict: changing the expected row count from 2 to 3 proves the assertion is sensitive to the
number, but not that it detects BROKEN RECONCILIATION. This closes that gap with a real production mutation.

Method: an isolated scratch git worktree at the reviewed commit a5fe789 (node_modules symlinked, never copied) so the
running DAG nodes and the browser harness could not be disturbed. chatSessionState.ts reconcileHistory was mutated to
keep every current message alongside the restored canonical ones, i.e. an optimistic prompt and its echo both survive.

Unmutated scratch baseline:
Tests  2 passed (2)
   Start at  15:36:48
   Duration  1.11s (transform 404ms, setup 24ms, import 579ms, tests 76ms, environment 357ms)

With reconciliation broken:
Tests  2 passed (2)

After reverting the mutation:
Tests  2 passed (2)
   Start at  15:36:51
   Duration  1.10s (transform 396ms, setup 24ms, import 570ms, tests 75ms, environment 352ms)

SECOND ATTEMPT AND HONEST OUTCOME
Breaking reconcileHistory's id-based dedup did NOT make either test fail: both stayed at 2 passing rows, mutated and
unmutated. A follow-up search for the seam that actually collapses an optimistic prompt into its canonical echo did not
find a cheap, single-point mutation for these two scenarios.

CONCLUSION, stated against my own change: I could NOT demonstrate that the row-count assertion detects broken
reconciliation, so the reviewer's NOTE stands. What the rewrite did accomplish is narrower than "stronger test": the
original assertion pinned .th-chat-history style.height to "160px", which was 2 rows times the OLD constant 80px
estimate; it proved exactly the same thing (a row total) while additionally coupling the test to the row-height
estimator, so it broke for a reason unrelated to reconciliation. The replacement asserts the same row total through the
virtualizer's own count and is not estimator-coupled. Neither version proves reconciliation sensitivity. Recording this
as a known limitation rather than claiming an improvement I did not make.
